    id: "investigate",
    title: "Investigate the options",
    summary: "A shopping list of eggs, milk and bread is your requirements, but at the shop you still choose the brand, the size and the kind: you investigate the options. In business terms, that's due diligence. The course's three steps: brainstorming, to find as many ways as possible to solve the problem (for Two Trees' space shortage: extend the factory, move to a bigger one, open another, or less obviously, drop some product lines or streamline manufacturing); benchmarking, internally with colleagues and externally with other companies, directly or through industry conferences and consultants, to find best practices and compare yourself with industry standards; and research, digging deeper into those options and looking for new or little-used ones, with consulting firms and universities as partners, or by publishing a request for proposals (RFP). Research might even reveal technology that uses space better and removes the need for a new building. Compare each option's costs, benefits and risks: ROI, NPV and payback, plus risk, complexity, sustainability, safety and regulatory compliance. The cheapest option isn't always best, and doing nothing is always an option.",
    figs: ["investigate-funnel"],
    terms: [
      ["Due diligence", "Investigating your options thoroughly before deciding, to make sure you're choosing the best one.", "Checking three warehouse sites' costs, road access and flood risk before signing a lease.", 0, "investigate-funnel"],
      ["Benchmarking", "Learning how others handle the same problem: internally with colleagues, or externally with other companies, directly or through conferences and consultants. It reveals best practices and how you compare.", "Asking another branch how it cut chilled-stock waste, or comparing your pick rates with an industry survey."],
      ["Request for proposals (RFP)", "A published request asking vendors to propose how they'd do the work and what they'd charge.", "Two Trees issues an RFP to three builders for a factory extension."],
      ["Do-nothing option", "Keeping things as they are. The course says it's always an option, and may be the best one if every alternative is risky or expensive.", "Holding off on a new warehouse until demand is clearer."]
    ],
